  "worked_solution": "First, note that $(\\frac{10}{4})$ means the same thing as ${}_{10}{C}_{4}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=10$ and $r=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-4)!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{6!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{\\cancel{6!}24} \\\\  & =210.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
